<!-- ============ 4 · APPROACH ============ -->
<section class="chapter" id="approach" aria-labelledby="h-approach">
  <header class="chapter-head">
    <span class="no">03</span>
    <h2 id="h-approach">Approach</h2>
    <img class="rail-art" src="/projects/art/stopwatch.png" alt="" loading="lazy">
  </header>
  <div class="chapter-body">
    <ol class="steps">
      <li class="step" data-reveal>
        <span class="step-no" aria-hidden="true">01</span>
        <div class="step-body">
          <h3>Gather from three public sources</h3>
          <div class="trio">
            <div><h4>TFRRS</h4><p>Race &amp; performance data</p></div>
            <div><h4>Athletic.net</h4><p>Athlete &amp; performance data</p></div>
            <div><h4>MileSplit</h4><p>Public performance data</p></div>
          </div>
          <figure class="viz" data-viz="merge">
            <div class="viz-stage" role="img" aria-label="Schematic: records from three public sources, TFRRS, Athletic.net and MileSplit, flow together into one combined dataset."></div>
            <figcaption><span class="viz-tag">Schematic</span>Three public sources, combined into one dataset</figcaption>
          </figure>
          <p>Rather than starting with one ready-made dataset, the workflow combined information from multiple public sources. The data had to be collected, organized, transformed, and checked before analysis.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li class="step" data-reveal>
        <span class="step-no" aria-hidden="true">02</span>
        <div class="step-body">
          <h3>Automate the collection</h3>
          <p>Python handled collection and organization as a repeatable, automated workflow. Automating it reduced repetitive manual entry and made large-scale data gathering practical.</p>
          <ul class="chips"><li>Python</li><li>Collection &amp; organization</li><li>Repeatable workflow</li><li>10,000+ data points</li></ul>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li class="step" data-reveal>
        <span class="step-no" aria-hidden="true">03</span>
        <div class="step-body">
          <h3>Clean, shape, and connect</h3>
          <div class="trio n4">
            <div><h4>Standardize</h4><p>Bring records from different sources into a consistent structure.</p></div>
            <div><h4>Clean</h4><p>Handle inconsistent, incomplete, or unusable values.</p></div>
            <div><h4>Transform</h4><p>Create analysis-ready variables and useful tables.</p></div>
            <div><h4>Match</h4><p>Connect records representing the same athletes across sources.</p></div>
          </div>
          <figure class="viz" data-viz="clean">
            <div class="viz-stage" role="img" aria-label="Schematic: misaligned records with inconsistent formats, a missing value and an unusable value are lined up and cleaned into one table, a new variable is added to every row, and records of the same athlete are linked."></div>
            <figcaption><span class="viz-tag">Schematic</span>The four steps above, on a few made-up records</figcaption>
          </figure>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li class="step" data-reveal>
        <span class="step-no" aria-hidden="true">04</span>
        <div class="step-body">
          <h3>Check before trusting</h3>
          <ol class="flow">
            <li>Raw records</li><li>Matched athletes</li><li>Validated records</li>
          </ol>
          <figure class="viz" data-viz="match">
            <div class="viz-stage" role="img" aria-label="Schematic: records from different sources, drawn as circles, squares and diamonds, link to the athlete they describe; each athlete's matched records are then checked."></div>
            <figcaption><span class="viz-tag">Schematic</span>Records from different sources link to one athlete, then get checked</figcaption>
          </figure>
          <p>Matching records across sources created another layer of quality control. The resulting fields and relationships were checked before the data was used in statistical analysis.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li class="step" data-reveal>
        <span class="step-no" aria-hidden="true">05</span>
        <div class="step-body">
          <h3>Model the large dataset</h3>
          <p>Once the data had been collected, cleaned, manipulated, matched, and validated, I used RStudio to analyze the large dataset and develop statistical models.</p>
          <ul class="chips"><li>RStudio</li><li>Python</li><li>Data validation</li><li>Statistical modeling</li><li>Data visualization</li></ul>
        </div>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 5 · FINDINGS ============ -->
<section class="chapter" id="findings" aria-labelledby="h-findings">
  <header class="chapter-head">
    <span class="no">04</span>
    <h2 id="h-findings">Findings</h2>
    <img class="rail-art" src="/projects/art/computer-cat.png" alt="" loading="lazy">
  </header>
  <div class="chapter-body">
    <p class="pull" data-reveal>Yes, it is a team sport, but how much depends on where you stand within the team.</p>
    <p data-reveal>A cross country team races seven runners, and only the top five count toward the team score. Comparing each runner's races on a team with their races unattached:</p>
    <figure class="lineup" data-reveal>
      <div class="lu-score" aria-hidden="true"><span>scoring five</span></div>
      <div class="lu-groups">
        <div class="lu-group is-top">
          <div class="lu-dots" aria-hidden="true"><i>1</i><i>2</i></div>
          <b>No significant difference</b>
          <p>Runners who consistently placed first and second on their team performed about the same on a team as unattached.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="lu-group is-mid">
          <div class="lu-dots" aria-hidden="true"><i>3</i><i>4</i><i>5</i></div>
          <b>The biggest difference</b>
          <p>The third, fourth and fifth runners saw the biggest difference between racing on a team and racing unattached.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="lu-group is-bench">
          <div class="lu-dots" aria-hidden="true"><i>6</i><i>7</i></div>
          <b>Don't score</b>
          <p>The sixth and seventh runners race, but their places don't count toward the team score.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <figcaption><span class="viz-tag">Schematic</span>A seven-runner team, by finishing position on the team</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <aside class="callout" data-reveal>
      <span aria-hidden="true">✦</span>
      <p><strong>Model results are only as reliable as the data going into them.</strong>That is why the preparation work, from collection through validation, was foundational to this project.</p>
    </aside>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 7 · REFLECTION ============ -->
<section class="chapter" id="reflection" aria-labelledby="h-reflection">
  <header class="chapter-head">
    <span class="no">05</span>
    <h2 id="h-reflection">Reflection</h2>
  </header>
  <div class="chapter-body">
    <p class="pull" data-reveal>Large-scale analysis starts long before the first model is fit.</p>
    <p data-reveal>Building a useful dataset required automation, careful cleaning, record matching, validation, and repeated checks for unexpected results. The research hit many roadblocks along the way, and reaching an answer meant working through each one.</p>
    <p class="lede" data-reveal>Those same habits now shape how I approach quality and reliability in software.</p>
  </div>
</section>

